Subject: META:  Debate on the List

Over 10 billion lines served...

This may be my first post here.  If so, I hope to make a good impression :-).

As someone who spends one or two weekends a month judging college debate
tournaments (I competed nationally for eight years, all through high school
and college), let me say that I think the quality of argumentation on
Extropians is pretty darn good.  Most of us don't spend hours in advance
composing what we're going to say, so the list takes on the spontaneous,
time-compressed nature of a debate.  Folks on this list refer to authority
when making factual claims (and in many cases _are_ an authority), express
their opinions freely and clearly label them as such, and interpret
opinions and facts with an advanced feel for the overall context of the
discussion.  Combine all this with the knowledge and the members' skill at
expressing themselves and you get a good argument flow.

The problem with debating rational vs. moral issues is that the two sides
have insufficient common assumptions about what is relevant.  Each interest
dismisses critical parts of the other, such as "morality" and "utilitarian
concerns", eliminating meaningful exchange.  Consider this:  how do you win
a philosophical argument?  Answer:  Ad hominem - matters of faith are
inherently undebatable, so you need to attack the person holding the views
instead of the views themselves.

