Subject: Extropian children

Andy:
> Hey, what's wrong with children having their own interests and
> philosophies? 

By definition people have their own interests and philosophies.
The trick is to mold our children's motivations fit well with our 
future interests.  That's the whole reason to have children in the 
first place, the biological motivation having been rendered "impotent and
obsolete" by birth control.   99% of families give in to the
cultural pressure to let their kids fly in the memetic winds, in which
cases I agree with the though others have expressed; it
wasn't in their best interests to have children in the
first place.  But with some forethought, a good channel blocker, home 
schooling, and the like it need not be that way.  Ask the Hutterites -- 
world champions both in fertility and memetic isolation, by no means 
coincidentally. 

To review some of the rational benefits of having children:

* Extend one's web of trust, a la the British and Chinese clans
cited by Duncan Frissel in his excellent article.  I've learned 
just recently yet again (a lesson I should have already known), 
that it's quite unwise to trust the judgement of somebody based 
on just mutual memetic interest.  The emotional bonds of a 
good family are needed for the strongest kinds of trust, along
with memetic agreement.  If either the long-standing emotional
ties or the memetic ties are missing, that strong trust breaks
down.  That's why it's so rare today -- cultural pressure
fights strongly against maintaining such a cohesive, unique
entity.  But once the memetic structures at play are recognized
for what they are, I think the disruption can be largely
overcome.

* A special but important case of the web of trust, creating a
network of people emotionally motivated to repair your cryonically
frozen (carc)ass.  When you're in deep freeze, your fate completely 
in someone else's hands, blood is thicker than perpetuity.   (*)

* Idealistic reasons 
-- the world is a better place with more smart, well educated people in it.  
-- the world is a better place with more Extropians.  In terms of future 
memetic impact, cultures like Islam with their explosively expansionary 
demographics will loom large in our futures.  Last year on sci.cryonics
a cryonicist freind of mine posted his dream of being reanimated into 
a world without violence, homophobia, and other current ills.  But if
Extropians get frozen without issue, the demographics
favor the opposite outcome:  you are much more likely to be
thawed out by a Mormon or Muslim than by an Extropian.

Despite these strong arguments favoring Extropian families, there
are a variety of practical reasons children are
quite unpopular among some Extropians.  Many Extropians are 
out of sorts with their own families, and some project that into 
hostility of the concept of family in general.   Many Extropians
find ourselves in a state of living where family
concerns are practically quite distant from our minds (me,
for example).

Extropianism is great for learning truths and the possible shapes
of our future, but not so good at motivating effective action in the 
present that might have the greatest impact on that future.
My hats off to Paul Whitmore's excellent essay pointing out that 
knowledge is useless without motivation, providing a good explanation
for what Tim May has called "future rapture".

For a single guy like me the Extropian grand visions are quite 
attractive.  But the practical nuts-and-bolts of building webs of 
strong, emotional trust are missing.  This may partially
explain why family men like Robin and Hal drift away, there
aren't many women on the list, etc.  I find the current 
thread refreshing in this regard, although it is still light
years away from addressing the complex issues of keeping together
a modern family.  Indeed, family concerns are so far away from 
Extropianism that I fear the two may never be reconciled, and
Extropianism may always operate on the castaways of other
memes.  When you get frozen take a copy of the Koran in with
you, or a few rosary beads; they may be your best ticket out.

Nick Szabo				szabo@netcom.com

(*) Come to think of it, a good perpetuity might be built around 
sociobiological N-out-of-M voting, with each relative holding a fraction 
of the key proportional to their genetic relationship.  This will
likely work much better for Extropian-raised children than it 
does for one's current crop of neanderthal anti-cryonics relatives;
some sort of "Extropian vow" might be tacked on for added memetic
assurance.




