From: carlf@media.mit.edu (Carl Feynman)
Subject: form, space and order

Mike Price writes:
>Carl Feynman laments:
>
>> Alas, we are stuck with our primitive monkey brains, only able to
>> visualize three [dimensions].
>
>Read a while back the opposite claim...This claim was made by someone
>who had played with a program that displayed the projection of a
>tesseract on the screen.  ... this guy claimed that after playing
>around with it for a few hours (I think) he found he could visualise in
>4-D.  He also claimed that this happened to other players.

When I was a kid, I was fascinated by four-dimensional geometry.  I spent
lots of time visualizing it, or trying to.  I got pretty good at thinking
about tesseracts, which are simple enough that you can develop a set of
heuristics for solving problems quickly.  For example, I solved all the
problems on the "4D geometry quiz" somebody posted here a day or two ago. 
I got all but one of them right, and none of them took me more than a few
seconds.  But that's because they were all about tesseracts, which I have
years of practice in thinking about.  And even so, I had to solve them all
by projecting the tesseract down to 3D in various ways.  I suspect this is
the ability that the guy quoted above has developed.

When I was younger and more pretentious, I told people that I could
visualize in 4D.  But I couldn't, and I doubt anyone else can.  I would
love to be proven wrong.  Meanwhile, for all you would-be multidimensional
thinkers out there, here's a non-tesseractal four-dimensional visualization
test:

1. Consider the four-dimensional polyhedron with five tetrahedra as its
faces.  Now chop off its corners deeply enough that the cutting spaces just
meet, to form a four-dimensional polyhedron with five tetrahedra and five
octahedra as its faces.  When you slice off one of the corners of this
shape, what shape is the intersection of the cutting space with the 4D
polyhedron?

2. Cover a hypersphere with an infinite number of circles of the same
radius as the hypersphere, such that every pair of circles is linked and
such that no two circles intersect.  (A good 4D visualizer should see this
immediately.  It took me days to visualize.)  Question a: Can you rotate
the sphere so that every point on every circle ends up on the same circle
after the rotation?  Question b: Can you rotate the sphere so that no point
on any circle ends up on the same circle after the rotation?  

Answers: 1: A triangular prism.  2a: Yes.  2b: No.

Anyone who can get these in a few seconds deserves to get to the
singularity before me.  Or perhaps is already there.


