   Charlie Stross writes:
   [...]
   > Note that the average cost of a state execution in the US was put at
   > about a million dollars by one program that I saw; the cost of keeping
   > a prisoner in jail is about $50,000 a year. So it's not, in principle,
   > much more expensive to run a British-style "serious" life imprisonment
   > system than to execute felons. 

   Let me tell you, a bullet doesn't any million dollars. That widely
   cited figure is the result of a legal system that delays carrying out
   the execution for 5 to 10 years of nearly constant appeals, all of
   which are paid for by the taxpayers. Need I say more?

With all due respect,  I wish people would cease and desist with the 
argument that capital punishment costs more than life imprisonment.  
That's predicated on a tottering house-o-cards that any sensible person 
would object to.  In our bass-ackwards system, convicts have more rights
than high school students.

Prisons should cost the taxpayers nothing.  They should turn a profit.
Just because someone's in jail doesn't entitle them to free food, shelter,
entertainment and smokes.  They should work just like the rest of us.
I object to capital punishment,  but in China, they make the executee's 
family pay for the bullets.  No reason we can't as well.

I like the three strikes and you're out idea,  but why not one strike and
you're out?  There is a certain degree of depravity beyond which ever
letting people back out of prison is madness.  An example: the bastard
who just took pot shots on the Long Island commuter train.  How can
you ever let this guy out?  Why is it that only Manson, who murdered no
one, is denied parole?

Andy

