By working hard, an honest craftsman can make a living and still do what
they want.  Examples: pianist/bandleader Cecil Taylor performs very 
inaccessible free jazz, yet makes as much as a good classical musician
by playing a lot of concerts;  drummer Bill Bruford finances his jazz
albums and tours by playing in the dinosaur supergroup Yes.

   >>Anyone got any ideas for media which can be worked in with minimal
   investment by a solitary specialist? I'm all ears.
   [...]

With the advent of desktop publishing and cheap digital recording,  the
only thing preventing a writer or musician from publishing their own
work is inertia.

This is an example of the benighted "blame the market for your own
mediocre products" argument found among middlemen in the entertainment 
and information industries.  But guess what?  With the advent of network 
transmission of books, records, movies, newspapers, etc., all these 
self-serving middlechumps are going to be out of business.  I can't 
wait ;-).

The fact that Gravity's Rainbow and Vineland by Thomas Pynchon were
bestsellers convinced me that the market is much more sophisticated
than the marketeers surmise.  I submit that it is the marketeers
themselves who are the wooly curmudgeons rather than the public who
are starving for quality.  As soon as these clowns have identified
a "target market" with a lowest common denominator,  they have
condemned their customers to another crappy product.

The bottom line here is that publishers who go for the maximum profit
are going to produce crap, and honest publishers who want to make a
living, but also produce something they can be proud of can still
operate.  Examples abound including Dover books, the REsearch series,
Black Sparrow Press, Grove Press, Calder Books, Bridge Records,
etc.

I have absolutely nothing against people who make money shoveling
lousy products to the intellectually starved masses like so much
excrement.  I just take issue with their circular logic in blaming
the market.  There is nothing in the axioms of free market economics 
that requires you to despise your customers.

