A few comments on this thread:

With regard to the "don't concede the argument" thread, I am reminded of my
father's comment that you can never persuade someone in an argument, you
can only provide him the arguments that he later uses to persuade himself."

If you lose an argument in the sense of being convinced with near certainty
that your position is wrong, you might as well concede it--but that rarely
happens. More commonly, there are reasons to believe something and reasons
not to believe it, and the balance shifts as you learn more during the
argument. The advantage to maintaining your position until the argument is
over is that it gives you a chance to see the other side's replies to all
the argument you can come up with on your side. The alternative is to yield
to your opponent's argument today, and only come up with the crushing
counter-argument tomorrow--when he is not around to point out why it is
wrong.

On the "personal data" side of gender differences, my wife and I do not fit
the parental stereotype. I have been an enthusiastic admirer of babies
(and, much more, of children) for as long as I can remember. Betty was
basically uninterested in babies until she had hers.

Someone, possibly in this thread, was suggesting interesting but
unfashionable books on gender difference and related issues. Let me add H.
L. Mencken, _In Defense of Women_. It is designed to infuriate both (all?)
sides to such discussions. My favorite Mencken quote on this issue,
possibly from that book, is "Women have simple tastes. They can take
pleasure in the conversation of babe's in arms and men in love."

On the point several people have made about mixed traits--I think Mencken
somewhere argues that all creative men are somewhat feminine.

